        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"partial\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": [\n    \"Human serum/plasma inosine levels were measured in RCC patients (CheckMate 025 trial, n=394 nivolumab) and stratified by median into high/low groups, correlating high levels with longer overall survival (33 months vs 22 months).\",\n    \"Specific quantitative concentration values (e.g., μM, ng/mL) for human serum inosine are not reported in the provided text, only hazard ratios and survival stratification.\",\n    \"Human tumor tissue inosine concentrations were not measured; instead, UBA6 protein expression in tumor tissue was used as a downstream proxy, with low UBA6 correlating with better ICI response.\",\n    \"Mouse plasma inosine levels were measured via untargeted metabolomics (244 metabolites), showing higher levels in tumor-bearing mice vs tumor-free, and reduction after ICB treatment, but specific values are referenced to Supplementary Figures not included here.\",\n    \"Mechanism identified: Inosine directly inhibits UBA6 in tumor cells, enhancing MHC-I expression and antigen presentation, sensitizing tumors to T-cell killing.\",\n    \"Contextual mention that gut microbiota influences purine metabolism and inosine levels, though SCFAs are only mentioned in the Introduction as known microbiota metabolites without new quantitative data in this study.\"\n  ],\n  \"auxiliary_material\": [\n    \"Inosine is described as a safe, naturally occurring purine used as a dietary supplement, suggesting repurposing potential.\",\n    \"Gut microbiota depletion with antibiotics compromised ICB efficacy and decreased purine metabolite levels in mice.\",\n    \"UBA6 expression levels vary by tumor type (high in B16/4T1, low in MC38), determining the efficacy of inosine supplementation.\",\n    \"Inosine half-life in vivo is approximately 15 hours, compared to adenosine's 30 seconds, impacting measurement stability.\"\n  ],\n  \"remaining_gap\"