ents (CheckMate 025). It states \"higher level of inosine was associated with the longer OS... (high: mOS = 33 months; low: mOS = 22 months)\".\n    - **Quantitative Data:** The text mentions \"plasma levels of 37/202 metabolites were associated with overall survival\". It mentions stratification by median (\"high and low are stratified by the median of each metabolite\").\n    - **Specific Concentrations:** Does it give specific μM or ng/mL values for inosine in human plasma or tumor tissue?\n        - Scanning the text: \"Fig. 1a Volcano plot showing the hazard ratios (HR) of high/low levels of serum metabolites...\". \"Fig. 1b Kaplan–Meier plot... grouped by the inosine level at the median of baseline level.\"\n        - The text *does not* explicitly state the actual concentration values (e.g., \"50 μM\") in the provided text. It refers to \"high\" vs \"low\" based on median stratification.\n        - It mentions \"plasma levels of inosine in B16-F0 tumour-bearing mice were higher than that in tumour-free mice (Supplementary Fig. 2f)\".\n        - It mentions \"plasma levels of inosine were reduced in both tumour-bearing mice and cancer patients after ICB treatment\".\n        - **Crucial Limitation:** The Discussion section states: \"Due to intact samples missing, we did not validate and perform the inosine measurement or transcriptomics analysis in our cohort [the 22 patient basket trial cohort].\"\n        - For the RCC cohort (CheckMate 025), it says they \"reanalysed the public dataset... regarding the metabolic profiles\". It doesn't explicitly list the concentration numbers in the text provided, just the association (HR, OS).\n    - **Tumor Microenvironment (TME) vs. Plasma:** The gap asks for concentrations *inside the human tumour microenvironment* OR serum. The paper provides *serum/plasma* data for humans (RCC). It does *not* provide direct measurement of inosine concentrations inside human tumor tissue (it measured UBA6 protein expression in tissue, 